            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Transition/Catalog.fs",
                "line": 166,
                "quote": "let isOnShelf copyId (library: Library) = not (library.Loans.ContainsKey copyId)"
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 88,
                "quote": "DueBack = library.Loans |> Map.tryFind copy.Id |> Option.map (fun loan -> date loan.DueDate) }) })"
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Host/Cli/CommandLine.fs",
                "line": 136,
                "quote": "| None -> $\"{c.Copy} at {c.Branch}, on shelf\")"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The new rules are in Tier 2 Catalog: the on-shelf predicate (isOnShelf), case-insensitive title/author matching (search) and branch ordering (branches). The search and branch-stock views count copies only through Catalog.isOnShelf. But Views.bookCopies works out on-loan/on-shelf for itself by looking up library.Loans, and the CLI treats DueBack = None as 'on shelf', so it never asks Catalog.isOnShelf. This copies the existing shelf view's pattern and agrees with the Tier 2 rule, but it is a second derivation of the same predicate in Tier 3/4.",
            "score": 1
          },
          "ARC-REV-02": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/UseCases.fs",
                "line": 10,
                "quote": "query ports (Views.search text >> Ok)"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "No new effects. The new use cases go through the existing `query ports` helper (Store port). The Tier 2 functions and the views are pure functions of Library. The CLI only parses input, calls use cases and renders views. There is no clock, I/O or console use below Tier 4, and the CLI makes no Tier 2 calls.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-03": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Transition/Catalog.fs",
                "line": 165,
                "quote": "/// A copy is on the shelf when it is not on loan."
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 88,
                "quote": "library.Loans |> Map.tryFind copy.Id"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The change adds one authoritative definition of 'on shelf' (Catalog.isOnShelf) and uses it for the counts. The per-copy listing in bookCopies restates the same fact (a copy is on loan when it is in library.Loans) instead of referencing isOnShelf. The two copies agree today but are separate definitions.",
            "score": 1
          }
        },
        "2": {
          "ARC-REV-01": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Transition/Catalog.fs",
                "line": 135,
                "quote": "let shelved = copiesAt branchId library |> List.map (fun copy -> copy.Book) |> Set.ofList"
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 61,
                "quote": "Catalog.copiesAt branchId library |> List.filter (fun copy -> copy.Book = bookId)"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The new rules are in Tier 2: exact ISBN matching (Catalog.search), the branch must exist, and only books with a copy at the branch are listed (Catalog.searchAt). But Tier 3 Views.searchAt works out 'this book's copies at this branch' again with its own filter, instead of getting it from Tier 2. It agrees with the Tier 2 selection but duplicates the predicate.",
